      <h1>Seven concepts to know</h1>
      <p>Short explanations and examples that connect protocol history to everyday web development.</p>
    </header>
    
      
        <section id="request"><h2>Request</h2><p>A client message that asks a server to perform an operation on a resource. It identifies a method and target, includes fields, and may carry content.</p><pre><code>GET /index.html HTTP/1.1
Host: example.edu</code></pre><p class="source-note">Illustrative HTTP/1.1 syntax. Source: <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a>.</p></section>
        <section id="response"><h2>Response</h2><p>The server’s answer to a request. It communicates the result and can return a representation such as HTML, JSON, or an image.</p><pre><code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html</code></pre><p class="source-note">Illustrative response headers. Source: <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a>.</p></section>
        <section id="method"><h2>Method</h2><p>The operation named in a request. GET retrieves a representation, HEAD requests the corresponding metadata without response content, and POST asks the resource to process submitted content. A method name describes intent, not a security guarantee. <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a></p></section>
        <section id="status"><h2>Status code</h2><p>A three-digit result code. The first digit groups the outcome: 1xx informational, 2xx successful, 3xx redirection, 4xx client error, and 5xx server error. Examples include 200 OK, 404 Not Found, and 503 Service Unavailable. <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a></p></section>
        <section id="headers"><h2>Headers</h2><p>Fields that carry metadata or control information. Content-Type identifies a media type, while Cache-Control communicates caching instructions. Headers allow HTTP to support additional behavior without replacing its core message model. <a href="references.html#rfc9110">[5]</a></p></section>
        <section id="tls"><h2>Transport Layer Security</h2><p>TLS establishes cryptographic protection between communicating endpoints. Modern HTTPS uses it to protect data in transit and usually authenticate the server. Certificates and session keys have different jobs: the certificate supports identity checks, while negotiated keys protect the data exchange. <a href="references.html#rfc8446">[6]</a></p></section>
        <section id="multiplexing"><h2>Multiplexing</h2><p>Allowing multiple streams to share a connection. HTTP/2 interleaves frames from separate streams over TCP. HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams, reducing cross-stream delivery blocking caused by lost data. Shared congestion and other constraints still affect performance. <a href="references.html#rfc9113">[3]</a> <a href="references.html#rfc9114">[4]</a></p></section>
